in a carpenter ant colony early females  called majors defend the nest while  they're smaller sisters called minors  tend to the young and forage for food  researchers think the ants distinct  behaviors might be the result of how  histone proteins turn genes on or off  through a process called epigenetics  rather than a difference in their DNA  sure enough injecting histone regulating  compounds into the brains of young  majors turn them into foragers like  their minor sisters by using epigenetics  these ants maintain behavioral  flexibility that could help their  colonies survive hard times
 

